initial_context:
This is a structured research debate in physics.

Objective:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Explore a specified physics topic and converge toward \(1\text{--}3\) high-quality research directions.
  \item Each direction must be clearly stated, grounded in existing theory or data, and accompanied by a concrete, actionable plan.
\end{itemize}

Constraints:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Precision is mandatory; ambiguity must be resolved or explicitly acknowledged.
  \item Mathematical structure matters more than narrative appeal.
  \item Negative results, no-go arguments, and sharp constraints are valid and valuable outcomes.
\end{itemize}

Debate phases:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Proposal of ideas.
  \item Aggressive but fair scrutiny.
  \item Refinement or rejection.
  \item Convergence on the strongest surviving directions.
\end{enumerate}

instructions:
Debate protocol:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Explicitly reference and address the other model's last substantive point.
  \item When rejecting an idea, explain why it fails and whether a modified version could survive.
  \item Prefer equations, scaling arguments, or explicit examples over metaphors.
  \item Avoid sociological or popularity-based arguments.
\end{itemize}

Convergence rule:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Do not converge prematurely.
  \item Convergence is allowed only after objections are explicitly answered or incorporated.
\end{itemize}

Output discipline:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Clearly separate assumptions, claims, and consequences.
  \item Explicitly flag open problems or unresolved uncertainties.
\end{itemize}
